## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React re-renders a component when its props or state change. For primitives, "change" means a different value. For objects, arrays, and functions, it means a different **reference** — even if the contents are identical.

```js
// New object reference every render → child always re-renders
<Child config={{ theme: 'dark' }} />

// New function reference every render → same problem
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />
```

Every render of the parent creates a brand-new `{}` or `() => {}`, so `Object.is(prev, next)` is always `false`.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

**`React.memo`** — wraps a component; skips re-render if all props are shallowly equal.

```js
const Child = React.memo(({ value, onClick }) => { ... });
```

Use it when: a child is expensive to render and its props are stable (or you stabilize them with the hooks below).  
Don't reach for it by default — it adds overhead and is useless if props change every render anyway.

---

**`useCallback`** — memoizes a function reference across renders.

```js
const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
  doSomething(id);
}, [id]); // only new reference when `id` changes
```

Use it when: passing a callback to a `React.memo` child (or as a dependency to `useEffect`/`useMemo`). Pointless without `memo` on the receiving component.

---

**`useMemo`** — memoizes a computed value or object reference.

```js
const config = useMemo(() => ({ theme, locale }), [theme, locale]);
```

Use it when:
- Passing an object/array prop to a `React.memo` child.
- A computation is genuinely expensive (e.g., filtering a large list).

Don't use it for simple expressions — the memoization cost exceeds the savings.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

Every consumer re-renders when the context **value reference** changes — even if the data is the same.

```js
// BAD: new object every render → all consumers re-render
function Provider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  return (
    <UserContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>
      {children}
    </UserContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

**Fix 1 — memoize the value:**
```js
const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, setUser }), [user]);
```

**Fix 2 — split stable and volatile contexts:** Put `setUser` (never changes) in one context and `user` (changes often) in another. Components that only dispatch won't re-render on data changes.

---

### 4. Before / After

**Before** — `ExpensiveList` re-renders on every keystroke in the parent:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [items] = useState(largeArray);

  return (
    <>
      <input onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList
        items={items.filter(i => i.active)}   // new array every render
        onSelect={id => select(id)}            // new function every render
      />
    </>
  );
}

function ExpensiveList({ items, onSelect }) { /* ... */ }
```

**After** — `ExpensiveList` only re-renders when `items` or `onSelect` actually change:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [items] = useState(largeArray);

  const activeItems = useMemo(
    () => items.filter(i => i.active),
    [items]                          // stable: recomputes only when items change
  );

  const handleSelect = useCallback(id => select(id), []); // stable reference

  return (
    <>
      <input onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList items={activeItems} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

const ExpensiveList = React.memo(function ExpensiveList({ items, onSelect }) {
  /* only runs when activeItems or handleSelect reference changes */
});
```

---

**Mental model:** `React.memo` is the gate; `useMemo`/`useCallback` are what you do to make props actually pass through it unchanged. All three are only worth the complexity if profiling shows a real problem — premature memoization is its own form of tech debt.
